s about a hundred  times a day right now we're right at the  head of the bed right like that's the  danger zone right that that's where most  of the droplet particles are going to be  if a patient sneezes or cost  we hear a lot about the need for  ventilators which are a precious  commodity right now but the other side  of that is that people who run those  machines and those would be respiratory  therapists so the safety and security of  those healthcare practitioners is  critical right now personally this is my  opinion and the weak link is going to be  the number of our case you know how many  we can we can bring in Behar says each  RT can take care of about four or five  patients on ventilators at any given  time but since the expectation is that  each Co vid 19 patient who requires  ventilation may need it for 10 days a  surge of cases at the same time would be  a major challenge he can only promise he  and his colleagues will do their best I  want to assure the the public that you  know no matter you know how big or small  the shortage of our T's is we will get  it done Paul Johnson Global News coping  through cooking cardio and card games  popular pandemic pastimes  if you're staying home because of the  Kovach 19 pandemic you are not alone  more than one-fifth of the world's  population is stuck within the four  walls of their homes either voluntarily  or by force  regi Cecchini reports on how we're  adapting to this new but temporary way  of life behind closed doors as Kovac 19  concerns impose social distancing on  billions of people the one thing we all  have in common is time and for many it  needs to be occupied so we're all on a  journey to figure out ways to make  better use of it like joining  celebrities offering a personal dance  party or trying to lose yourself page  after page after page keeping our  routine as much as you can it's really  great isolation life has turned our  homes into a map as we try to search for  something new this is an opportunity  again for us to exhibit some patience in  a fast food and takeout world many  people have forgotten the simple art of  preparing a meal cooking is really I say  a sensual experience you taste it you  touch it and smell it you see it but  when you can connect with people you  know the experience you have around it I  think that's what's really brought  people together now it's not just  ingredients that are testing our  creative sides there's one an abstract  painting there was one on making plastic  sculptures the Vancouver Art Gallery  helping people turn their living room  into a virtual studio museums do more  than preserve the past through objects I  can we play a very important role in  shaping the future in our digital  offerings well other famous museums  bring their collections to a virtual  audience  finding child care in a world of social  distancing can also be a challenge so  kids are now learning the ups and downs  of being home more often others are  getting lost in the world of  make-believe we are probably cozied up  at home with your family library systems  like the one in Calgary offering story  time and a chance to keep students  interested in reading companies are also  taking advantage of people's free time  some putting computer science coding  classes into the palms of future coders  hands  and as the world watches the seconds  tick by waiting for some kind of good  news our homes provide us with new  opportunities to reconnect with  ourselves and our families and who knows  self-isolation  could also lead to a boom in 2033 we may  have a whole new group of teenagers  known as Generation quarantine Reggie  teeny Global News Washington that is  global national for this Saturday i'm  colleen christie tonight we leave you  with a new version of Canada's favorite  pastime call it isolation hockey a group  of friends put together this  penalty-free socially distance game  passing toilet paper from coast to coast  to coast hope it's not the